{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path('/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed218530468/3/codegen_prompts/7.md')\nprint(p.read_text())\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed218530468/3`\n- 题号：`7`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed218530468/3/7.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"7\">\n- `description`：通过对比两个相似图案的差异像素来确定\"印章\"形状，再将印章盖印到网格中标记点指定的位置。\n- `Input structure`：背景为 Black。网格被一条 Azure 色的水平分隔线分为上下两个区域。上方区域包含两个相同大小的矩形色块（由同一颜色如 Green 绘制的相同图案），它们左右并列放置，两个图案几乎相同但有若干像素位置不同——其中一个图案在这些位置是 Green，另一个在这些位置是 Black（或反之）。下方区域是一片较大的由某种颜色（如 Yellow）绘制的图案，其中散布着若干 Red 单像素标记点。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 比较上方两个图案，提取它们的差异：将左图案有色而右图案为 Black 的像素位置集合作为\"印章形状\"（以差异像素的最小外接矩形为基准进行裁剪）。\n  - 对下方区域中的每个 Red 标记点，以该标记点为印章的左上角锚点，将印章形状叠印到下方图案上：印章覆盖的位置全部变为 Magenta 色。\n  - Red 标记点本身也被 Magenta 覆盖。若印章超出下方区域边界则只画出区域内的部分。\n  - 上方区域和 Azure 分隔线保持原样不变。\n- `Key insight`：核心挑战在于解题者需要先通过\"找不同\"提取印章形状（差异检测层），然后理解 Red 标记点是印章的锚定位置（位置编码层），最后执行批量盖印操作。印章不是直接给出的，而是隐藏在两个近似图案的差异之中。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/57edb29d, train/5adee1b2, train/e9fc42f2, eval/2d0172a1, eval/0934a4d8, train/652646ff, train/b71a7747, eval/247ef758\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"57edb29d\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3583\nmechanism=0.4350\nlexical=0.0124\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:axis, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, output:crop, relation:overlap, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/57edb29d.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/57edb29d.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtract the single plain rectangle and redraw it with the aligned rectangles' border-relative marker pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The boundary color is the background/separator.\n- Foreground objects are dense rectangular regions; exactly one rectangle has only its base color.\n- Other rectangles contain their base color plus marker pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop the only plain rectangle; this crop is the output canvas.\n- Use marked rectangles whose row span overlaps the plain rectangle's row span.\n- Transfer their marker pixels by preserving each marker run's distance from the nearest rectangle edges.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marked rectangles are examples of an edge-relative pattern, so the missing plain rectangle receives the same pattern resized to its own dimensions.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"edge-relative marker run s in a marked rectangle aligned with the plain rectangle row band\",\n  \"dst\": \"run d in the plain rectangle crop\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.nearest_edge_offsets == d.nearest_edge_offsets\",\n  \"transform\": \"paint each matched d with the shared marker color; keep base rectangle fill elsewhere\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same shape as the plain rectangle\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5adee1b2\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3242\nmechanism=0.3672\nlexical=0.0124\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:closed_region, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/5adee1b2.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/5adee1b2.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nUse the left-edge color legend to draw a padded rectangular halo around each matching object while preserving enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- One or more two-row markers sit on the left edge; each marker is a 2x2 block whose left column is an object color and right column is its halo color.\n- Away from the markers, every source color from the legend appears as one or more 4-connected monochrome objects.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input pixels unchanged.\n- For each source-color object, take its tight bbox expanded by one cell and clipped to the grid.\n- Recolor background pixels in that expanded region to the marker's halo color, except for background holes enclosed by the object.\n\nKey insight:\n- The small left-edge bars are a legend: they map object colors to the colors used for the surrounding padded bbox.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected monochrome objects on the background plus left-edge two-column color legend markers\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each object whose color appears in the legend, fill background pixels in its one-cell padded bbox with the paired legend color; preserve the object and any enclosed background holes\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"left-edge two-column legend maps object color to bbox-fill color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e9fc42f2\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3472\nmechanism=0.3893\nlexical=0.0123\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, output:crop, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e9fc42f2.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e9fc42f2.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nAssemble the foreground objects by docking equal-colored boundary markers edge-to-edge.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and several 4-connected multicolor foreground\nobjects.  One foreground color is the body color; the less frequent foreground colors are\nboundary connector markers, and each marker color appears once as a free end or twice as a\nmatched docking pair.\n\nRule\nCrop each foreground object to its bounding box and translate the objects so that the two\noccurrences of every paired connector color touch on their exposed boundary sides.  Preserve\neach object's orientation and colors, then return the tight crop around the assembled\nforeground on the original background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe colored marker pixels are ports: matching colors specify which objects must touch, and\nthe marker's side of its object determines the direction of the translation.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected foreground object sprite s with boundary connector markers\",\n  \"dst\": \"matching boundary connector marker d on another sprite\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.marker.color == d.marker.color and the two marker pixels have opposite exposed sides\",\n  \"transform\": \"translate sprites so each paired marker touches edge-to-edge; preserve sprite colors and orientation\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"tight crop of the assembled foreground on the original background color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3332\nmechanism=0.3982\nlexical=0.0120\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:closed_region, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, output:crop, relation:direction, relation:overlap, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/2d0172a1.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/2d0172a1.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.\n- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.\n- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.\n- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.\n- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.\n\nKey insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"rough two-color nested foreground drawing: closed frame objects and small blob objects\",\n  \"dst\": \"canonical frame-and-dot diagram preserving containment and sibling row/column order\",\n  \"transform\": \"replace each blob by one pixel; replace each frame by a rectangular border padded around its direct children; keep outside blobs outside the enclosing frame\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"canonical crop; root-level outside blobs occupy a 3x3 bg slot\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"0934a4d8\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3213\nmechanism=0.3984\nlexical=0.0118\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, output:crop, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/0934a4d8.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/0934a4d8.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nReveal the rectangular patch hidden by the solid marker using the grid's reflection symmetries.\n\nInput structure:\n- A square colored pattern with one solid rectangular marker color occluding a patch.\n- The visible pattern has horizontal and vertical reflection symmetry on a large lower/right strip.\n- The top and left outer strips also expose transpose counterparts for cells not recoverable by those reflections.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique color whose full extent is a solid rectangle; this rectangle is the output region.\n- Infer the reflected strip start as the smallest row/column offset whose lower/right strips are mirror-consistent, ignoring marker pixels.\n- For each marker pixel, copy the unique non-marker value in its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; if that orbit is entirely hidden in the outer strip, use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not part of the pattern; it covers a missing rectangle whose cells are still determined by the visible mirror or transpose copies elsewhere in the grid.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"solid rectangular marker over a mirrored color pattern\",\n  \"dst\": \"hidden marker-sized rectangle\",\n  \"transform\": \"replace each marker pixel by the matching pixel from its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart when that orbit is also hidden\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"crop to marker bbox\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"652646ff\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3314\nmechanism=0.4134\nlexical=0.0117\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, output:crop, relation:direction, relation:overlap, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/652646ff.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/652646ff.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecover each partially hidden 6x6 ring sprite and stack complete copies from top layer to bottom layer.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Several non-background colors are visible fragments of the same 12-pixel\n  outline ring in a 6x6 box; small off-template marker pixels are noise.\n- Ring fragments overlap, so upper rings replace some expected pixels of\n  lower rings, and a ring may also be clipped by the input boundary.\n\nRule:\n- Identify every substantial color whose pixels all lie on one translated\n  copy of the ring template.\n- For each missing in-bounds template pixel, the color currently occupying\n  that pixel is above the hidden ring.\n- Topologically sort these occlusion relations from top to bottom, then draw\n  one complete 6x6 ring for each color in that order on a background canvas.\n\nKey insight:\n- The output is not a crop of the scene; it is the layer stack of complete\n  ring sprites reconstructed from their visible fragments.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"visible fragments of overlapping 6x6 outline-ring sprites; sparse off-template colors are marker noise\",\n  \"dst\": \"vertical stack of one completed ring sprite per recovered color\",\n  \"transform\": \"complete every fragment to the 12-pixel ring template using the input background\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"order sprites from top layer to bottom layer: if a color occupies another sprite's expected ring pixel, it is placed earlier\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"6 columns; height is 6 times the number of recovered ring sprites\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b71a7747\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2987\nmechanism=0.3649\nlexical=0.0130\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:output_resize, object:axis, object:rectangle, object:sprite, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/b71a7747.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/b71a7747.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtract the hidden two-color image by compacting the rows and columns of its complete mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid contains one pair of colors whose pixels occupy every cell in the\ncross product of their active rows and active columns; all other colors form\nthe surrounding filler or separators.\nRule\nFind that unique color pair, keep exactly the rows and columns touched by\nthe pair, and return the induced subgrid.\nKey insight\nThe target image is not chosen by color id; it is the only two-color mask\nwhose occupied pixels form a full rectangular row/column product after\ndeleting unused rows and columns.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"unique two-color mask whose pixels fill the cross product of its active rows and active columns\",\n  \"transform\": \"delete every row and column not touched by that mask, preserving the induced two-color subgrid\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"active mask rows x active mask columns\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3136\nmechanism=0.3693\nlexical=0.0126\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/247ef758.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/247ef758.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"sprite s in the left area whose color appears on the right frame\",\n  \"dst\": \"center at positions d in the right frame\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.same_row_frame_edges.color and s.color == d.same_col_frame_edges.color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"move sprites from bottom to top, with new covering old\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n## 必读参考代码路径\n\n执行前请至少快速阅读这些文件的 `main()` 和 `generate_input()`，重点看它们如何避免退化输入、如何随机放置对象、如何验证隐式假设：\n\n- `/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/57edb29d.py`\n- `/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/5adee1b2.py`\n- `/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e9fc42f2.py`\n- `/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/2d0172a1.py`\n- `/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/0934a4d8.py`\n- `/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/652646ff.py`\n- `/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/b71a7747.py`\n- `/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/247ef758.py`\n\n## 实现要求\n\n1. `main(grid)` 必须实现新题描述中的确定性转换规则，不能只识别 `generate_input()` 的固定模板。\n2. `generate_input()` 必须随机生成多样样例，不能固定死。随机维度应覆盖：网格大小、对象数量、位置、颜色、方向、形状参数或关键分支。\n3. 生成器必须保证输入满足 `main()` 的隐式假设：对象不重叠、不越界、特殊标记唯一或可区分、匹配关系无歧义、关键分支出现概率相同。\n4. 禁止把 ARC 颜色 id 、亮度、拼写等当作人类不可见的数值语义参与排序、大小比较或隐藏映射。颜色变化必须有可见结构线索。注意颜色亮度、拼写等也是不可见信息，若描述中出现违规的颜色id或者亮度，可自行使用少量的固定颜色替代，但注意生成时要在三个以内输入输出网格中对所有变换100%覆盖度。\n5. 输出样例要视觉清晰：避免对象粘连、覆盖关系混乱、同一像素多重语义、过密导致规则不可读。\n6. 若规则有多个分支，生成器不能长期固定成一个分支；应让分支在随机样例中合理出现，即每个分支出现的概率平均，所谓的分支是在描述中提到的分支，即规则语义中的不同情况。\n7. 代码风格参考 `ARC_code`：使用 `numpy`，必要时使用本地 helper，但目标文件应能被 `SourceFileLoader` 直接加载。\n8. 可以在目标目录放公共 helper 文件，但若只实现一道题，优先保持目标文件自包含，减少额外依赖。\n9. `main(grid)` 必须带一段 docstring，按 `ARC_code` 参考题的格式分四部分书写：首行用一句话概述转换目标；随后 `Input structure:` 描述输入网格中的对象/背景/标记/边界/组件；`Rule:` 给出从输入到输出的确定性变换规则；`Key insight:` 点明解题核心洞见。该 docstring 供下游相似度/索引流程（按 `main` 的 docstring 抽取机制标签）使用，缺失会导致此题无法被收录为参考题。\n10. 网格尺寸必须符合 ARC 规范：`generate_input()` 产生的输入网格、以及 `main()` 返回的输出网格，行数和列数都必须 ≤ 30（即任何一维都不超过 30，整体不超过 30×30）。随机化网格大小时把上界封顶在 30；若规则会放大网格（如平铺、加边框、整数倍缩放），必须保证放大后的输出仍 ≤ 30×30，必要时收紧输入尺寸或放大倍数的随机范围，确保 100% 的随机样例都不越过 30×30。\n\n## 写入要求\n\n将完